chapter = '# CHAPTER 238\n\n## THE SEALER\n\nThe pages for The Missing Key were still on the table in the morning.\n\nThat did not make them a schedule.\n\nI knew this because Teren had said, "Again tomorrow if it stays paired," which contained both tomorrow and if, and only one of those words was dangerous.\n\nLyssa was already gone.\n\nThe small paper-wrapped thing from last night was gone too. I noticed because it had been beside her elbow, then stopped thinking about it because objects were allowed to leave our apartment without giving testimony.\n\nThe brown cloth had not exercised that right.\n\nThree pins.\n\nSame fold.\n\nMy damaged shirt remained on the shelf with three loose threads.\n\nAt least somebody respected continuity.\n\nI ate bread, drank water, and read the Messenger pages once.\n\nNo knock came.\n\nI waited until the bread was gone.\n\nStill no knock.\n\nTheatre had taught me many things, most of them about waiting in the correct place until somebody wanted me. Debt had taught me the opposite.\n\nI folded the pages and left them on the table.\n\nIf the play stayed paired, Rinna knew where I lived.\n\nIf it did not, I had saved myself a walk.\n\nEither way, Antonius Vale still existed.\n\nThe morning was colder than yesterday and brighter. Carrow had the hard clean light that made chipped plaster and old paint look newly accused. A cart of cabbages blocked half the lower lane. The driver was asleep on the seat while three people moved around him with the patient hatred of people who had decided waking him was somehow more trouble.\n\nI went east.\n\nMy body had recovered normally from the theatre day. Right hand fine. Shoulders fine. Right leg fine. Residual limb comfortable. Left crutch tip dull and trustworthy.\n\nThe Vale office was already open.\n\nThe ink-fingered clerk looked up when I came in.\n\n"Bundles?" I asked.\n\n"Later."\n\n"Acknowledgments?"\n\n"Done."\n\n"Excellent. I have successfully arrived for nothing."\n\nHe pointed to the far table.\n\n"Seals."\n\nI looked.\n\nA shallow tray held folded outer wrappers, a squat wax pot near a little lamp, two brass stamps, a stack of account copies, and enough string to hang a very small criminal.\n\n"What am I sealing?"\n\n"Outgoing account copies."\n\n"Why?"\n\nHe stared at me.\n\n"Because open paper is less private than closed paper."\n\n"Strong system."\n\n"Sit."\n\nI sat.\n\nThe chair was the same one I had used before, which meant I already knew which rear leg complained and how far my crutches could lean without falling into the passage.\n\nThe clerk put six packets in front of me.\n\n"Not all of them."\n\n"Good."\n\n"Practice row."\n\n"Less good."\n\nHe showed me the work.\n\nEach packet already had the account copy inside its outer wrapper. My job was not to decide the account, correct the amount, or interpret any mark I did not know. I checked the outer name and house mark against the top line visible before the final fold. If they matched, I folded the wrapper closed along an existing crease, tied it once around with string, then waited.\n\nThe waiting mattered.\n\nThe clerk checked the face of the packet and put a short ink stroke beside the house mark.\n\nOnly then did I seal the string knot with wax and press the plain office stamp.\n\n"Not the other stamp," he said.\n\nI looked at the second brass stamp.\n\nIt had a deeper cut and a small edge mark.\n\n"What does that one do?"\n\n"Not your work."\n\n"Efficient training."\n\n"Good."\n\nI looked at him.\n\nHe did not care.\n\nThe first packet went badly.\n\nNot catastrophically.\n\nThat would have been interesting.\n\nI used too much wax.\n\nThe drop spread under the stamp, squeezed past the knot, and made a red-brown lump with one recognizable edge and the general dignity of stepped-on fruit.\n\nThe clerk looked at it.\n\nI looked at it.\n\n"Private," I said.\n\n"Ugly."\n\n"Still private."\n\nHe took a narrow knife, lifted the wax before it fully hardened, and handed the packet back.\n\n"Less."\n\n"Technical language remains strong here."\n\n"Less."\n\nI used less.\n\nThe second seal looked like a seal.\n\nThe third looked almost competent.\n\nOn the fourth packet I got ahead of myself.\n\nName matched.\n\nHouse mark matched.\n\nFold.\n\nString.\n\nWax.\n\nThe clerk caught my wrist before the stamp came down.\n\nNot hard.\n\nEnough.\n\n"No stroke."\n\nI looked at the packet.\n\nHe had not checked it yet.\n\n"Right."\n\n"Why?"\n\n"Because your check and your authority are different things."\n\nHe took his hand away.\n\nI disliked how cleanly that sentence fit the office.\n\n"I matched it."\n\n"You did."\n\n"And I still wait."\n\n"Yes."\n\nHe checked the face, added the ink stroke, and pushed it back.\n\nI stamped.\n\nNo lesson music played.\n\nThe packet joined the others.\n\nBy the end of six, the seals were all recognizable and none of them had become attached to the table.\n\nThe clerk gave me twelve more.\n\nApparently I had been promoted from dangerous to useful enough.\n\nThe office filled around the work.\n\nA woman came in with two account slips and argued about a date. Not loudly. She had the controlled anger of somebody who had already explained herself somewhere else and resented having to own the story again.\n\nThe ink-fingered clerk listened, compared her slips to a ledger, then called another clerk over.\n\nNeither asked me.\n\nGood.\n\nA man carrying a narrow crate wanted someone named Sel.\n\nNobody named Sel was there.\n\nHe left the crate anyway.\n\nA boy came for three sealed packets and was told two.\n\nHe took two.\n\nAnother man stood near Antonius\'s door for almost ten minutes before being admitted and spent the whole time pretending not to look at anyone else.\n\nCommerce.\n\nI kept tying string.\n\nThe work had a rhythm once my hands stopped trying to improve it.\n\nCheck name.\n\nCheck house mark.\n\nFold.\n\nTie.\n\nWait.\n\nInk stroke.\n\nWax.\n\nStamp.\n\nSet aside.\n\nThe clerk checked faster than I worked. That meant I never had to wait long enough to become philosophical.\n\nExcellent design.\n\nI was on the second stack when I found a problem.\n\nThe outer wrapper said MARRIS HOUSE, SOUTH WALL.\n\nThe visible top line inside said MARRIS HOUSE, SOUTH WALK.\n\nOne letter.\n\nDifferent mark below it too.\n\nI stopped.\n\nThe names matched.\n\nThe amount was none of my business.\n\nThe house did not.\n\nI turned the packet sideways.\n\nThe clerk came over.\n\n"What?"\n\nI pointed.\n\nHe read it.\n\nThen pulled the account copy free.\n\n"Wrong wrapper."\n\n"Do I win?"\n\n"No."\n\n"Do I at least avoid losing?"\n\n"Continue."\n\nHe found the correct wrapper in the stack below.\n\nNo hidden conspiracy.\n\nNo meaningful connection.\n\nSomebody had put one paper inside another paper.\n\nThe world survived.\n\nI tied the correct packet.\n\nNear midday, a boy arrived asking for me.\n\nThis one I knew.\n\nNot his name.\n\nHis function.\n\nRinna\'s handbill boy from yesterday.\n\nHe stood in the office doorway looking uncomfortable with the amount of paper surrounding him.\n\nThe ink-fingered clerk looked at him.\n\n"Business?"\n\n"Theatre."\n\nThe clerk looked at me.\n\nI closed my eyes briefly.\n\nOf course.\n\nThe boy said, "Rinna says Teren kept the piece. If you\'re coming, come now."\n\nThere it was.\n\nThe if had changed.\n\nNot completely.\n\nEnough.\n\nI looked at the stack in front of me.\n\nSeven packets left in the row.\n\nAnother stack behind it.\n\nThe wax pot warm.\n\nMy hands already in the work.\n\n"Performance?"\n\nThe boy shrugged.\n\n"Rehearsal now. Show later."\n\n"Messenger?"\n\n"She said same."\n\nThe ink-fingered clerk had gone back to his ledger.\n\nI said, "Can I leave this and come back?"\n\nHe looked at the wax.\n\nThen at the packets.\n\n"Today?"\n\n"Yes."\n\n"No."\n\nNot cruel.\n\nNot threatening.\n\nThe answer of a man with paper that had to leave today.\n\n"If I go?"\n\n"Someone else finishes."\n\n"Credit?"\n\nHe looked at me.\n\nI regretted asking it that way.\n\nNot because money was shameful.\n\nBecause I had accidentally asked him to invent a rule for my feelings.\n\n"Antonius credits work completed," he said.\n\nThere it was.\n\nNo speech.\n\nNo punishment.\n\nNo promise that my partial stack was worthless either.\n\nThe theatre boy shifted in the doorway.\n\nI looked at him.\n\n"Tell Rinna I\'m working."\n\n"You aren\'t coming?"\n\nI looked at the seven packets.\n\nThen at the wax.\n\nThen at the boy.\n\n"No."\n\nHe nodded.\n\nNo disappointment.\n\nNo argument.\n\nHe left.\n\nThe door closed.\n\nI sat still for one breath longer than necessary.\n\nThe clerk said, "Wax."\n\nI looked down.\n\nThe drop on my knife was cooling.\n\n"Right."\n\nI reheated it.\n\nThat was the choice.\n\nNot dramatic enough for a play.\n\nDebt credit instead of eight lines and three entrances.\n\nI expected to feel more certain after making it.\n\nI did not.\n\nGood.\n\nPeople who felt certain every time they chose between two useful things were probably lying or rich.\n\nI continued.\n\n\nThe next packet had a house mark I recognized from an earlier acknowledgment stack.\n\nThat recognition was almost useful.\n\nMy hand started toward the string before I had checked the name.\n\nI stopped it.\n\nDifferent family.\n\nSame house mark.\n\nI read the line again.\n\nThe clerk saw me pause.\n\n"Problem?"\n\n"No."\n\nHe waited.\n\n"I knew the mark and almost let that do the rest."\n\nHe nodded once.\n\nNot praise.\n\nNot correction either.\n\nI finished the comparison properly and folded the packet.\n\nA few minutes later the boy who had come for two sealed packets returned with one of them.\n\nHe held it by the edge.\n\n"Cracked."\n\nThe wax had split across the center.\n\nMy seal.\n\nI knew it because the stamp sat slightly off level.\n\nThe clerk took it, bent the wrapper gently, and the wax opened along the crack.\n\n"Bad?"\n\nI asked.\n\n"Broken."\n\n"Cause?"\n\nHe looked at the thin seal.\n\n"Too little."\n\nI stared at him.\n\n"This morning you said less."\n\n"Less than too much."\n\nThe office had found a philosophical position after all.\n\nHe scraped the old wax away, handed me the packet, and watched while I resealed it with a slightly larger drop.\n\n"That?"\n\n"Yes."\n\nThe boy took it.\n\nNo one recorded my journey from too much wax to not enough wax and back toward mediocrity.\n\nCivilization would have to survive without the chart.\n\n\nThe woman with the date problem left with one corrected slip and the same expression she had entered with.\n\nThe crate for Sel disappeared while I was looking at wax.\n\nAntonius came out once, saw me, saw the sealed packets, and went back into his room.\n\nI received no medal.\n\nBy early afternoon my right hand had begun to feel the fine repetitive work differently from crutch use. Thumb and forefinger warm from string. A small wax smear dried near one nail. Shoulders fine because I had been seated. Right leg stiff enough that I stood during a natural break and walked the length of the room twice.\n\nThe clerk did not interpret this.\n\nNeither did I.\n\nI drank water.\n\nSat again.\n\nFinished the row.\n\nThen the stack.\n\nThe last packet had a thick fold and fought the string.\n\nI pulled harder.\n\nThe knot shifted sideways.\n\nThe clerk looked over.\n\n"Again."\n\n"It is tied."\n\n"Badly."\n\n"It has commitment."\n\n"Again."\n\nI untied it.\n\nThe second knot sat flat.\n\nWax.\n\nStamp.\n\nDone.\n\nThe clerk counted the finished packets without asking me to count with him.\n\nThen carried them to Antonius.\n\nI cleaned the knife and put the string ends into the scrap bowl because leaving them everywhere was apparently how offices became theatres.\n\nAntonius came out with the account book.\n\nHe opened it to my line.\n\nI saw the current figure before he wrote the new credit.\n\nStill not small.\n\nSmaller after.\n\nHe wrote.\n\nClosed the book.\n\n"Useful?"\n\nI asked.\n\nHe looked at the sealed packets.\n\n"They are leaving."\n\nThis family of answers had infected the city.\n\n"Credit recorded?"\n\n"Yes."\n\n"Good."\n\nAntonius\'s eyebrow moved.\n\nI regretted nothing.\n\nOutside, afternoon had already started bending toward evening.\n\nI could have gone home.\n\nInstead I went to the theatre.\n\nNot because I thought they needed me now.\n\nThat possibility had already been answered by time.\n\nI wanted to know.\n\nThe front doors were open and people were coming out.\n\nSo the performance had happened.\n\nThat answered one question before I entered.\n\nInside, the house was becoming a room again.\n\nAudience voices thinning.\n\nBenches visible.\n\nRinna at the side with coins and a small ledger.\n\nNessa carrying two costume pieces toward the rear passage.\n\nJori lifting the brown door frame with Davin.\n\nThe Missing Key had survived.\n\nI had not been required.\n\nFor one stupid second that hurt.\n\nThen Marek came offstage with the brass-colored key in one hand.\n\nHe saw me.\n\n"Dead."\n\n"What?"\n\n"You died."\n\n"I was at Vale."\n\n"Debt death."\n\n"Was the play good?"\n\nHe considered.\n\n"Hara was."\n\nAccurate.\n\n"Messenger?"\n\nMarek pointed with the key.\n\n"Venn."\n\nGood.\n\nVenn existed.\n\nThe world had not waited for my body to become available.\n\nNessa reached Marek and held out her hand.\n\nHe put the key into it immediately.\n\nShe looked at me.\n\n"You missed coat."\n\n"I noticed."\n\n"Venn fit."\n\nI looked at the green coat over her arm.\n\n"Better?"\n\n"Different."\n\nThat was probably the kindest answer available.\n\nTeren came down the house aisle.\n\nHe saw me.\n\n"Vale?"\n\n"Yes."\n\n"Fine."\n\nI waited.\n\nHe kept walking.\n\nI turned.\n\n"That\'s it?"\n\nHe stopped.\n\n"What do you want?"\n\n"I don\'t know."\n\n"Then yes."\n\nFair.\n\n"Did Venn do Messenger?"\n\n"Yes."\n\n"Good?"\n\n"He left."\n\nThat landed exactly where he intended it to.\n\nI smiled despite myself.\n\n"Excellent."\n\n"Tomorrow, different pair."\n\n"So The Missing Key is done?"\n\n"For now."\n\nNo punishment.\n\nNo reclaimed role.\n\nNo schedule.\n\nIt had run because it was useful today.\n\nI had not been there.\n\nSomebody else had done the work.\n\nThat was theatre too.\n\nRinna finished with the ledger and came over.\n\n"The boy found you."\n\n"Yes."\n\n"You stayed."\n\n"Yes."\n\n"Vale?"\n\n"Credit."\n\nShe nodded.\n\n"Good."\n\nI pointed at her.\n\n"No."\n\nShe almost smiled.\n\nNo theatre pay waited for me because I had not done theatre work.\n\nThis also seemed offensively fair.\n\nI helped nobody with strike.\n\nNot because I was angry.\n\nBecause the workday I had actually chosen had already used my hands, and Jori and Davin had the frame.\n\nI said goodbye to nobody formally.\n\nMarek was arguing with Pell about whether a key could be considered missing if the wrong key had been found.\n\nHara had taken off the repaired shoe and was rubbing one heel through her stocking.\n\nNessa had the green coat.\n\nTeren had another set of pages.\n\nThe theatre continued.\n\nI left.\n\nOn the way home I bought a small hot pastry filled with mashed roots.\n\nI paid for it.\n\nNo debt arithmetic.\n\nI had just spent the day reducing the debt and was still allowed to be hungry.\n\nThe pastry burned my tongue.\n\nJustice.\n\nAt home, Lyssa was there before me.\n\nThe brown cloth remained near the wall.\n\nThree pins.\n\nSame fold.\n\nMy damaged shirt remained on the shelf.\n\nThree loose threads.\n\nThe paper-wrapped item was not on the table.\n\nThe beans were still wherever beans went when they acquired plans.\n\nLyssa looked at the empty space in my hands.\n\n"Theatre?"\n\n"Yes."\n\n"Show?"\n\n"Yes."\n\nShe waited.\n\n"Without me."\n\nThat got her attention.\n\n"Why?"\n\n"Vale had work."\n\n"Good?"\n\nI sat down.\n\n"Depends who you ask."\n\n"Who?"\n\n"Vale credited me."\n\nShe nodded.\n\n"Theatre replaced me."\n\nAnother nod.\n\nNeither answer seemed to trouble her.\n\nI took bread.\n\n"Venn did it."\n\n"Did what?"\n\n"Messenger."\n\nShe had already lost interest in the administrative structure of my disappointment.\n\nProbably healthy.\n\nI ate.\n\nAfter a while she said, "You chose."\n\n"Yes."\n\n"Want different?"\n\nI thought about the green coat.\n\nThe warm wax.\n\nTeren saying fine.\n\nThe account figure getting smaller.\n\nHara probably being good without me.\n\n"No."\n\nLyssa nodded.\n\nThen reached for my pastry.\n\nI moved it.\n\nHer hand followed.\n\n"You ate," I said.\n\n"So did you."\n\n"Earlier."\n\nShe looked at me.\n\nI had made a tactical error.\n\nI tore the pastry in half.\n\nShe took the larger piece.\n\nOf course.\n\nI ate mine before the office could send anyone to seal it.'

